#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151842 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/id #uuid "c638e469-4c15-52f3-a397-a7aac86d654e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14588" :question/problem "If $ka \\equiv kb \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\,7)$\nis equivalent to $%\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\:(\\textrm{mod} \\,7)$,\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k$?\n\n1. $k=-12$\n2. $k=4$\n3. $k=-14$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=7$. Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=-12$, then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(-12,7) = 1$. Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=-12$.\n- If $k=4$, then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(4,7) = 1$. Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=4$.\n- If $k=-14$, then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(-14,7) = 7\\neq 1$. Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-14$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151843 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca70f6c-d087-5b8e-aff2-581adca9ed1f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the First Division Property"}] :question/id #uuid "9f2134e8-62dd-5748-a202-819b4ca5208f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14589" :question/problem "Given that $4a \\equiv 4b \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n2. $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,2)$\n3. $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n4a \\equiv 4b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,8) =4\n$$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4a & ≡4b & (mod8) \\\\ 4 \\cdot a & ≡4 \\cdot b & (mod4 \\cdot 2) \\\\ 4 \\cdot a & ≡4 \\cdot b & (mod4 \\cdot 2) \\\\ a & ≡b & (mod2)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151858 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3822141e-3cea-5c34-b925-a522c2273335" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Division Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "344211e7-11c0-54f9-8a78-e64ff53deff0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14612" :question/problem "Given that $15a \\equiv 15b \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\, 20)$\n2. $a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n3. $7a \\equiv 7b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$" :question/worked-solution "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\neq 0$, we have\n$ka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k, n) = 1$, we have\n$ka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}15a & ≡15b & (mod20) \\\\ 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a & ≡5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot b & (mod5 \\cdot 4) \\\\ 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot a & ≡5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot b & (mod5 \\cdot 4) \\\\ 3a & ≡3b & (mod4)\\end{bmatrix}$\nSince $\\gcd(3,4)=1$, by the second property, we have:\n$3a \\equiv 3b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n- Statement III is true. By statement II and the second property, we have:\n$a≡b(mod4)⟺7a≡7b(mod4)$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8273ffaf-d449-5879-b48f-a97ed0faa8a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149415} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a94429-2cd6-5a8f-b755-f1199f52fd11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not coprime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149416} {:answer/id #uuid "bd2e7d97-21ad-5b11-8477-addc91b68f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149417} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3063f2-5a9d-590f-aecd-6b02388bef27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "positive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149418} {:answer/id #uuid "67d662b7-a564-5584-98e3-98796541e510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "coprime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149419}] :answer-field/id #uuid "242b01bf-8d86-5045-ac95-60b2e452c51a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149414} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd213bc1-9a42-5bd3-9dc3-c216e38dd848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is equivalent to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149421} {:answer/id #uuid "dd65fbc6-a940-51a4-bae0-7b39b0748efd" :a